                <pb facs="0656" n="571" sameAs="frus:frus1919v02/pg571"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">812.00/22893</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Mexico</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Summerlin</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Acting Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-06-27"><hi rendition="#italic">June 27,
                            1919</hi></date>.<lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received July
                        14.</hi>]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 2123</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to the Department’s
                    telegraphic instruction of June 7, 4 P.M.,<anchor n="50" xml:id="d530-f1"/>
                    relative to the protection of the American Mormon Colonies in the Casas Grandes
                    District, I have the honor to report that the Embassy is today in receipt of a
                    second note from the Foreign Office, dated June 24, 1919, which states that the
                    military authorities have been instructed by telegraph, to afford due protection
                    to American citizens and their properties at Casas Grandes and in the Mormon
                    Colonies.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">George T.
                    Summerlin</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
